Topic 4.3: Cross-Axis Field Modulation and Warp Drive Hardware (การควบคุมตัดฉากข้ามแกน และฮาร์ดแวร์วาร์ปไดร์ฟ)
[English]
To manipulate spatial distances or time vectors on the perpendicular $Z$-axis (Time/Distance to Mars), the system bypasses direct $Z$-axis input. Instead, it utilizes an orthogonal cross-field vector drive deployed across the accessible horizontal $X-Y$ plane. High-energy DC pulses are fired in an axisymmetric, opposing pattern within the horizontal $X-Y$ induction ring. Following the right-hand rule of electromagnetism, the crushing horizontal force vectors cancel each other out but generate a massive, localized net pressure spike that explodes or implodes vertically along the perpendicular $Z$-axis.
Space Contraction Mode ($+Z$): Firing $X-Y$ pulses out-of-phase ($0^\circ$) forces local space-time grids to collapse downward into a localized gravity well, pulling distant forward coordinates (Mars) closer to the rig.
Space Expansion Mode ($-Z$): Firing $X-Y$ pulses in-phase ($180^\circ$ reinforcement) generates an artificial repulsive field, inflating the space-time fabric behind the rig to push it forward with a high safety margin.
