Topic 4.4: High-Frequency Closed-Loop Telemetry to Mars (ระบบโทรมาตรลูปปิดความถี่สูงสู่ดาวอังคาร)
[English]
To establish a stable data-link window across 225 million kilometers from Earth to Mars, the rig operates on a high-frequency switching mechanism. First, it harvests the highly volatile Interplanetary Magnetic Field (IMF) and solar winds. This energy runs through a Cosmic Bandpass Filter to strip out irregular noise, storing clean DC energy in High-Capacitor Cores. The controller then utilizes a high-frequency PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) drive in the kHz/MHz range to inject Hi-Freq DC Pulses into the $X-Y$ grid.
These rapid fires create ultra-sharp Rising/Falling Edges. The high-frequency transient spikes oversaturate the local Cosmic Filter, opening a microsecond window known as a "Spatial Slit". The distance along the $+Z$ axis contracts dynamically, bringing the Mars telemetry profile directly to the Earth receiver for instant sampling without travel time. Meanwhile, the Closed-Loop Feedback Control reads field distortions in real-time. If Back-EMF or spatial resistance surges, a Dual-Interlock system (Ultra-Fast Energy Relief Shunt and High-Transient Breaker) trips instantly to dump residual energy safely into space, protecting the rigid solid-state field framework and preserving the vessel's structural integrity.
